# Copy to android/key.properties and fill in. That file is gitignored, as are
# *.jks and *.keystore — the upload key must never enter this repository.
#
# Only needed for a LOCAL release build. Codemagic supplies the same four values
# as CM_KEYSTORE_PATH / CM_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD / CM_KEY_ALIAS / CM_KEY_PASSWORD
# from the keystore uploaded to it, and app/build.gradle.kts prefers those.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE UPLOAD KEY FOR EVERY MGKCODES ANDROID APP, Lift included. It already
# exists — Run created it on 2026-09-10 — so there is nothing to generate here.
# **Do not make a Lift-specific one**; a second key would mean a second secret
# to lose for no gain, and Lift has no Play listing yet to be locked to either.
#
#   keytool -genkey -v -keystore mgkcodes-upload.jks \
#     -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 -alias upload
#
# Codemagic holds it as `mgkcodes_upload`, which both Android workflows declare.
# This workflow declared `liftio_upload` until 2026-09-10 and no keystore of
# that name had ever been uploaded — so it would have failed on its first run,
# silently, because it has never had a listing to publish to.
#
# Play uses Google Play App Signing: this key signs the UPLOAD, and Google holds
# the key that signs what users install. Losing it is recoverable — Google can
# reset it — but only through a support request, so treat it as precious rather
# than irreplaceable.
#
# `storeFile` is an absolute path, or one relative to android/app/.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

storeFile=/absolute/path/to/mgkcodes-upload.jks
storePassword=
keyAlias=upload
keyPassword=
